-- Pins what a space next to the $p (new paragraph) and $n (new line)
-- output markers does to the game output.
--
-- The author's question, in short: is
--
--     "You walk up to the big wooden door. $pAnd when you open it..."
--
-- different from
--
--     "You walk up to the big wooden door.$pAnd when you open it..."
--
-- Short answer, as pinned below: a space *before* the marker is
-- swallowed and makes no difference, while a space *after* the marker
-- survives and indents the following line. A newline in the source text
-- counts as a space, so a marker sitting at the end of a source line is
-- the same as writing a space after it.
--
-- Each case below is one output statement. It starts with a tag so the
-- cases can be told apart in the output, and it is prefixed with $$ to
-- suppress the space Alan would otherwise insert between two strings,
-- so that the tag lands flush left and any leading space in the output
-- belongs to the case itself.
--
-- Note that only the *leading* space is visible in a diff -b comparison,
-- which is what the shell 'regr' script uses. 'make test' runs jregr,
-- which compares literally.

The l Isa location
End The l.

Start At l.


-- $p, the new paragraph marker

"P-A abc.$pdef$p"                 -- no space at all, the reference case
"$$P-B abc. $pdef$p"              -- space before the marker
"$$P-C abc.$p def$p"              -- space after the marker
"$$P-D abc. $p def$p"             -- space on both sides
"$$P-E abc.  $pdef$p"             -- two spaces before the marker
"$$P-F abc.
   $pdef$p"                       -- marker first on the next source line
"$$P-G abc.$p
   def$p"                         -- marker last on a source line
"$$P-H abc.$p" "def$p"            -- marker last in the string
"$$P-I abc. $p" "def$p"           -- ditto, with a space before it


-- $n, the new line marker

"$$N-A abc.$ndef$p"               -- no space at all, the reference case
"$$N-B abc. $ndef$p"              -- space before the marker
"$$N-C abc.$n def$p"              -- space after the marker
"$$N-D abc. $n def$p"             -- space on both sides
"$$N-E abc.  $ndef$p"             -- two spaces before the marker
"$$N-F abc.
   $ndef$p"                       -- marker first on the next source line
"$$N-G abc.$n
   def$p"                         -- marker last on a source line
"$$N-H abc.$n" "def$p"            -- marker last in the string
"$$N-I abc. $n" "def$p"           -- ditto, with a space before it


-- A space between two consecutive markers

"$$X-A abc.$p$pdef$p"             -- reference case
"$$X-B abc.$p $pdef$p"            -- space between the two markers
"$$X-C abc.$n$ndef$p"             -- reference case
"$$X-D abc.$n $ndef$p"            -- space between the two markers
"$$X-E abc.$n $pdef$p"            -- space between markers of both kinds


-- The author's original example, spelled out

"$$D-A You walk to the west up to the big wooden door. $pAnd when you open the door...$p"
"$$D-B You walk to the west up to the big wooden door.$pAnd when you open the door...$p"
